 ## Why the carrier name alone is not a moisture specification
 
-A separate study of spray-dried elderberry pomace extract illustrates the transfer problem. Its all-MD formulation had a reported moisture content of 5.63 ± 0.15%, compared with 4.10 ± 0.09% for all-GA—the reverse of the endpoint ordering in the freeze-dried clove study.[2][1] The elderberry experiment used a different extract, a stated carrier-to-extract-dry-mass ratio of 1:2 and spray drying at an inlet temperature of 120 ± 1 °C.[2]
+A separate study of spray-dried elderberry pomace extract illustrates the transfer problem. Its all-MD formulation had a reported moisture content of 5.63 ± 0.15%, compared with 4.10 ± 0.09% for all-GA—the reverse of the endpoint ordering in the freeze-dried clove study.[2][1] The elderberry experiment used a different extract and spray drying at an inlet temperature of 120 ± 1 °C.[2] Its methods contain an unresolved ratio discrepancy: the drying section states 1:2 carrier:extract dry mass, while the assay section uses 1:6 extract:carrier to calculate expected compound concentration.[2] The moisture results are cited as reported; neither ratio is used here to calculate a carrier fraction or normalize performance.
 
 This is not evidence that GA reverses its inherent behavior, or that one drying method caused the difference: several features changed between experiments. It is evidence against transferring a finished-powder moisture ranking solely from the carrier names. The elderberry moisture results are also not a replication of seven-day humidity uptake.
 
